description:
crate-jmap-tasks-server/src/memory.rs:224-227:

    #[derive(Clone, Default)]
    pub struct MemoryBackend {
        inner: Arc<Mutex<Inner>>,
    }

The derived Clone semantics: every clone() call produces a new MemoryBackend struct that holds the SAME underlying Arc<Mutex<Inner>>. Mutations through one clone are visible to all clones. This is the right behaviour for the use case (the type is meant to be shared across a dispatcher's many handlers). But \`derive(Clone)\` is a misleading signal:

  - Convention in idiomatic Rust: \`#[derive(Clone)]\` on a struct named *Backend usually means \"cheap copy of independent state\" or \"deep copy if needed\". A reader doing a 30-second glance does not expect \"all clones share mutex-protected state\".

  - A consumer writing test fixtures may write code like:

        let backend = MemoryBackend::new();
        let snapshot = backend.clone(); // expected: snapshot of current state
        // mutate `backend` ...
        // inspect `snapshot` ... <-- WRONG: snapshot reflects current state, not the cloned point in time

    They will be surprised. The bug surfaces only when their test happens to mutate-and-compare in the wrong order.

The semantics ARE documented in the type-level rustdoc:

    /// Cloning is cheap: every clone shares the same underlying \`Arc<Mutex<…>>\`.

…but only on the doc, not in the type system. Three contract-strengthening options:

  1. Manual Clone impl with a doc that hammers the shared-state semantics (current rustdoc moves there).

        impl Clone for MemoryBackend {
            fn clone(&self) -> Self {
                Self { inner: Arc::clone(&self.inner) }
            }
        }

     Reader sees the explicit Arc::clone and the docstring on the impl block. No surprise.

  2. Newtype the Arc explicitly to make the shared-state aspect type-visible:

        pub struct MemoryBackend {
            inner: SharedBackendState<Inner>,
        }

     where SharedBackendState<T> = Arc<Mutex<T>> with its own Clone impl whose rustdoc says \"clones share the same underlying state\".

  3. Add a separate fn snapshot(&self) -> MemoryBackend that DOES deep-copy, and tell callers to use it when they want independent state. Pair with the manual Clone impl from option 1.

Option 1 is the lowest-cost fix that closes the contract footgun. Option 3 is additive.

Workspace propagation: the Arc<Mutex<Inner>> pattern is used identically in every sibling MemoryBackend (jmap-mail-server, jmap-calendars-server, jmap-chat-server, jmap-contacts-server, etc.). A workspace-wide sweep is in order; file once and propagate.
